<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Han Zhao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">304–329</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Han Zhao was a state of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> founded in 304 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yuan">Liu Yuan</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> leader, at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> period. Calling his state Han to claim the legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, he and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-cong">Liu Cong</a> destroyed the Western Jin, sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 311 and taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 316. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yao">Liu Yao</a> renamed it Zhao in 319, and it was destroyed by the rival Later Zhao in 329.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/han-zhao.jpg" alt="Han Zhao" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixteen_Kingdoms_317_AD_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The founders of Han-Zhao came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a>, who had been resettled across the northern frontier in 50 CE and served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> as a buffer against nomadic enemies. After their government dissolved in 188, the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> reorganised the remaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> in 216 into the Five Divisions, confined to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fen-river">Fen River</a> valley in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, where they served as cavalry. Their elites adopted the surname Liu, since Xiongnu rulers had made marriage alliances with the ruling Liu clan of the Han. Liu Yuan, son of the commander Liu Bao, was sent as a hostage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and brought up in Confucian scholarly culture. Traditional history makes him a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu Chanyu</a>, but modern scholars have questioned this.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 304, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-eight-princes">War of the Eight Princes</a>, the Five Divisions offered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yuan">Liu Yuan</a> leadership. He was hailed as Grand Chanyu and then became King of Han, declaring that he would restore the fallen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. Famine forced him to move his capital several times, and after 308 it was settled at Pingyang. He died in 310. His eldest son, Liu He, was killed within a week by his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-cong">Liu Cong</a>, who took the throne.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-cong">Liu Cong</a> the Han army, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-le">Shi Le</a>, destroyed the Jin imperial army and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 311, capturing Emperor Huai of Jin in the Disaster of Yongjia. Liu Yao, a cousin, crushed the Jin court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> five years later. Both captured emperors were made to serve as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cupbearer">cupbearers</a> before being executed. The state was nonetheless weak, since generals outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, above all Shi Le, acted as independent warlords. After Liu Cong died in 318, the consort kinsman Jin Zhun killed his successor in a coup.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yao">Liu Yao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-le">Shi Le</a> put down the coup, and Liu Yao moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>. When he had Shi Le's envoy executed, Shi Le declared independence and founded the Later Zhao. In 319 Liu Yao renamed his state Zhao and honoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu Chanyu</a> in the ancestral temple. War between the two Zhao states began in 324. In 328 Liu Yao was captured at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and executed, and in 329 his son Liu Xi was driven from Chang'an and killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The regime used Chinese offices such as chancellor and grand marshal beside a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> structure of military and aristocratic government. The Chanyu Platform, headed by the Grand Chanyu, held military power and managed the affairs of minority tribes, but ranked below the emperor. This dual administration reinforced the separation between Hu and Han peoples, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> states later adopted it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yao">Liu Yao</a> also opened an Imperial University at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> taught by Confucian scholars.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Han-Zhao” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the War of the Eight Princes (291–306) wrecked the Western Jin, the Xiongnu-led <b>Han Zhao</b> state sacked Luoyang in 311, capturing Emperor Huai, and took Chang'an in 316, capturing Emperor Min.</blockquote><a href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They contributed to the transformation of northern China that culminated in the Sixteen Kingdoms period of the fourth and fifth centuries, when Liu Yuan, a Xiongnu noble, founded the <b>Han Zhao</b> state in 304, and his successors sacked Luoyang in 311.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>He captured the <b>Han Zhao</b> ruler Liu Yao in 328, destroyed his state in 329 and declared himself emperor in 330.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-zhao">Later Zhao</a></li></ul></section>
